 "avoidBefore": "all",
 "energy": "Routines last under 3 minutes at near-maximal effort with tumbling, stunts and jumps, so power and anaerobic capacity lead, backed by enough aerobic fitness to recover across long practices.",
 "summary": "Cheer asks bases to squat, press and catch a person overhead, and flyers to balance, twist and land from height; most injuries happen in stunts, and concussion is the most common single injury. The plan builds leg and overhead strength, landing, ankle and wrist capacity, and trunk stiffness, and sets strict concussion and stunting rules.",
 "evidence": [
  {
   "claim": "Stunts caused 60% of cheerleading injuries and 96% of concussions and closed head injuries; spotting or basing caused a third of stunt injuries, and the ankle was the most injured body part.",
   "source": "Shields, Fernandez & Smith 2009, Journal of Athletic Training",
   "url": "https://nata.kglmeridian.com/view/journals/attr/44/6/article-p586.xml"
  },
  {
   "claim": "In US high schools, concussion was the most common cheer injury (31%), and cheer had one of the highest shares of injuries costing 3 or more weeks out.",
   "source": "Currie et al. 2016, Pediatrics",
   "url": "https://news.cuanschutz.edu/news-stories/cheerleading-among-safest-high-school-sports-but-injuries-can-be-severe"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Paediatric guidance: remove any cheerleader with signs of head injury and do not let them return without written medical clearance; give bases and spotters strength and conditioning; keep stunts off hard or uneven surfaces.",
   "source": "Brenner et al. (American Academy of Pediatrics) 2012, Pediatrics",
   "url": "https://profiles.wustl.edu/en/publications/cheerleading-injuries-epidemiology-and-recommendations-for-preven/"
  }
 ],
